{"alignments":[{"id":"alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c","layer":"CN","nodeId":"cnm_h072","reason":"Canonical description and mastery criteria are unchanged from this pinned native topic; placement remains a separate source annotation.","relation":"exact","reviewProvenance":{"comparedFields":["description","criteria"],"reviewMeaning":"Definition identity, not an official curriculum equivalence claim.","sourceRevision":"62b341d4b66b175413d6e3f697e37639fd96aa2d","type":"source_identity_comparison"},"reviewStatus":"machine_content_review","sourceNodeId":"cnm_h072","status":"accepted"}],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 4/7","reason":"Factoring gives x-intercepts (-5,0) and (3,0), while B=(0,-15). The two angles beside the upward vertical at B have tangents 5/15=1/3 and 3/15=1/5. Therefore tan(∠ABC)=(1/3+1/5)/(1-(1/3)(1/5))=4/7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-angle-addition","label":"Add the angles beside the vertical","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-angle-addition":["cnm_234","cnm_276","cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072","cnm_234","cnm_276"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A","reason":"The quotient (tan α + tan β)/(1 - tan α tan β) is undefined when its denominator is zero. The individual tangent values must also be defined.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["A is one listed failure condition; the individual tangent values must also exist."],"questionId":"cnm_h072_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_017","cnm_h069"],"id":"m1","label":"Check the denominator","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072","cnm_017","cnm_h069"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2","reason":"Writing s = tan A + tan B and p = tan A tan B gives 1 = tan45° = s/(1 - p). Thus s + p = 1 and (1 + tan A)(1 + tan B) = 1 + s + p = 2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1985_AMC_12_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h071","cnm_276","cnm_277","cnm_146"],"id":"tangent-addition","label":"Use the 45-degree sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-addition":["cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072","cnm_h071","cnm_276","cnm_277","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Native reconciliation under the recorded task assumptions; no blind-review claim.","derivedAnswer":"C, pi/4","reason":"The given product gives b=1/3. Let alpha=Arctan(1/2) and beta=Arctan(1/3). Both lie in (0,pi/2), so their sum lies in (0,pi). Its tangent is (1/2+1/3)/(1-1/6)=1. The only such angle in that interval is pi/4.","recordedAnswer":"C","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1979_AMC_12_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"addition-formula-and-branch","label":"Compute the tangent of the sum and select its branch","methodDirectNodeIds":{"addition-formula-and-branch":["cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h069","cnm_h058"],"id":"tangent-sum","label":"Find the tangent and select the angle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-sum":["math:inverse-tangent-and-solutions","cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["math:inverse-tangent-and-solutions","cnm_h072","cnm_h069","cnm_h058"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 2","reason":"Let n=tan θ. Then m=tan 2θ=2n/(1-n²), while m=4n. Nonhorizontality gives n≠0, so 4(1-n²)=2 and n²=1/2. Hence mn=4n²=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1980_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-angle-doubling","label":"Tangent angle doubling","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-angle-doubling":["cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h165","cnm_h069"],"id":"tangent-addition","label":"Translate the angles into slopes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-addition":["cnm_h166","cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h166","cnm_h072","cnm_h165","cnm_h069"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 45°","reason":"For the acute angle θ, tan θ=|(2-1/3)/(1+2/3)|=1. Therefore θ=45°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"inclination-difference","label":"Tangent of inclination difference","methodDirectNodeIds":{"inclination-difference":["cnm_h072","cnm_277"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072","cnm_277"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-of-inclination-difference","label":"Subtract the inclination angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-of-inclination-difference":["cnm_h166","cnm_h072","cnm_277"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h166","cnm_h072","cnm_277"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 14","reason":"Let angle DAB=θ. Then cos(2θ)=2/3, so tan θ=1/sqrt(5), tan(2θ)=sqrt(5)/2, and tan(3θ)=7/sqrt(5). Thus CD/BD=tan(2θ)/(tan(3θ)-tan(2θ))=5/9, giving m+n=14.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1998_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"multiple-angle-trigonometry","label":"Use the doubled angle and tangent addition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"multiple-angle-trigonometry":["cnm_276","cnm_h073","cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_276","cnm_h073","cnm_h072"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 75°","reason":"Set B=(0,0), D=(1,0), and C=(3,0). Since ∠ABC=45°, write A=(t,t). The 60° direction of DA gives t=sqrt(3)(t-1), so t=(3+sqrt(3))/2. Therefore tan(∠ACB)=t/(3-t)=2+sqrt(3)=tan(75°). The angle is acute, hence it is 75°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"right-triangle-trigonometry","label":"Resolve 45° and 60° directions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"right-triangle-trigonometry":["cnm_278","cnm_277","cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_278","cnm_277","cnm_h072"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 50/3","reason":"Let x=AD/BD and y=DE/BD. The geometric progression gives y²=(y²-x²)/(1-x²y²), hence y=1 since x,y>0. The arithmetic progression becomes 2(1+x)/(1-x)=1+1/x, giving x=1/3. Since BE²=2BD²=100, the area is AD·BD=xBD²=50/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-sum-and-difference","label":"Tangent identities and progression relations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-sum-and-difference":["cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_276","cnm_174","math:reciprocal-trig-identities"],"id":"angle-ratios-and-progressions","label":"Translate the progressions into tangent equations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"angle-ratios-and-progressions":["cnm_h203","cnm_h209","cnm_h072","cnm_165","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h203","cnm_h209","cnm_h072","cnm_165","cnm_p148","cnm_137","cnm_276","cnm_174","math:reciprocal-trig-identities"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 20","reason":"Since tan 15°=tan(45°-30°)=2-√3, CE=10(2-√3), so DE=20-CE=10√3. Thus AE²=AD²+DE²=100+300=400, giving AE=20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_10A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-difference-and-pythagoras","label":"Tangent subtraction and Pythagoras","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-difference-and-pythagoras":["cnm_278","cnm_h072","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_278","cnm_h072","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (3/2)","reason":"Write the slopes as m and 6m, and put u=|m|>0. The angle condition gives 5u/(1+6u²)=1, so (3u−1)(2u−1)=0. The attainable products 6u² are 2/3 and 3/2; the greater is 3/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12B_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-difference","label":"Tangent of the angle difference","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-difference":["cnm_h166","cnm_h072","cnm_277","cnm_224"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072","cnm_224","cnm_h166","cnm_277"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, -1","reason":"With t=tan x, tan(2023x)=Im((1+it)^2023)/Re((1+it)^2023). The denominator has constant term 1. The numerator's t^2023 coefficient is Im(i^2023)=-1, since 2023 is congruent to 3 modulo 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-addition-recursion","label":"Repeated tangent addition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-addition-recursion":["cnm_h072","cnm_h071","cnm_h200"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072","cnm_h200","cnm_h071"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: β=π/2-2α","reason":"cos(2α)=1-2(3/5)²=7/25=sin β. Since α<π/4, both 2α and β are acute, so β is the complement of 2α.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12A_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-angle-addition","label":"Compare tangent using angle addition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-angle-addition":["cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 13 units","reason":"The sides are 14, 6+t, and 8+t, with semiperimeter 14+t. Since K=4(14+t), Heron's formula gives 16(14+t)^2=48t(14+t), so t=7. The sides are 13, 14, and 15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1953_AMC_12_Q50","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"half-angle-tangents","label":"Use tangent ratios of half-angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"half-angle-tangents":["cnm_276","cnm_h072","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_276","cnm_h072","cnm_115"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 14; the coordinate sum is 3/11","reason":"Let u,v be the endpoints of the slope-2 side and w the third x-coordinate. Then u+v=2. The other slopes are (2+√3)/(1-2√3) and (2-√3)/(1+2√3), whose sum is -16/11. They also sum to u+v+2w=2+2w, so w=-19/11. Thus u+v+w=3/11 and m+n=14.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2005_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sixty-degree-side-directions","label":"Rotate side directions by 60 degrees","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sixty-degree-side-directions":["cnm_h166","cnm_h072","cnm_277"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h166","cnm_h072","cnm_277"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 0","reason":"Set a_n=tan(πr_n/12), with r_1=3, r_2=2, and r_(n+2)≡r_n+r_(n+1) modulo 12. The initial pair returns after 24 terms, no residue is 6, and r_17=0, so the recurrence has no poles. Since 2009≡17 modulo 24, |a_2009|=0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-angle-residue-cycle","label":"Use tangent addition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-angle-residue-cycle":["cnm_h072","cnm_h064","cnm_h069","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072","cnm_h064","cnm_h069","math:modular-congruence"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, (50 - 25sqrt(3))/2","reason":"The equal legs have length 5, so the altitude from C to AB is h=5/sqrt(2). Symmetry places the two trisectors 15 degrees on either side of this altitude, giving DE=2h*tan(15 degrees). Hence the area is h^2*tan(15 degrees)=(25/2)(2-sqrt(3))=(50-25sqrt(3))/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_10A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"symmetry-and-tangent-fifteen","label":"Use symmetry and tan(15 degrees)","methodDirectNodeIds":{"symmetry-and-tangent-fifteen":["cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_278","cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_278","cnm_h072"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 2√3-3 square inches","reason":"The square has side 1. HL congruence of triangles DMC and BNC gives AM=AN=t. Equilateral side equality gives 2t²=1+(1-t)², so t=√3-1. Subtracting the three corner right triangles from the square gives area 1-[t²+2(1-t)]/2=2√3-3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1974_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"fifteen-degree-triangle","label":"A 15° right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"fifteen-degree-triangle":["cnm_058","cnm_276","cnm_277","cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_276","cnm_277","cnm_h072"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (912/119)","reason":"The x-axis is the other external tangent. Its intersection with the required tangent lies on the line of centers, giving H=(-38/5,0). The center line has slope 5/12 and bisects the tangent angle, so m=2(5/12)/(1-(5/12)²)=120/119. Thus b=-m(-38/5)=912/119.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12A_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-angle-bisector","label":"Tangent angle bisector","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-angle-bisector":["cnm_263","cnm_h167","cnm_276","cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_263","cnm_h167","cnm_276","cnm_h072"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 15","reason":"The original slope is 3/5, and (20,20) lies on the line. After the counterclockwise rotation, the slope is ((3/5)+1)/(1-(3/5))=4. Thus k has equation y-20=4(x-20), whose x-intercept is 15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12A_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"rotate-the-inclination-angle","label":"Use the tangent addition formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"rotate-the-inclination-angle":["cnm_245","cnm_h171","cnm_h166","cnm_h072","cnm_h169","cnm_202"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h171","cnm_h166","cnm_h072","cnm_h169","cnm_245","cnm_202"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 150°","reason":"Let the equal sides be s and the base angles be α<45°. Then b=2s cos α and h=s sin α, so s^2=2bh gives 1=4 sin α cos α=2 sin(2α). Since 0<2α<90°, 2α=30°, and the vertex angle is 180°-30°=150°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12B_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"pythagorean-ratio-equation","label":"Pythagorean ratio equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pythagorean-ratio-equation":["cnm_174","cnm_221","cnm_h072","cnm_277"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_221","cnm_h072","cnm_277"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"pythagorean-side-ratio","label":"Find the ratio of height to half-base","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pythagorean-side-ratio":["cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_221","cnm_h072","cnm_278"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_174","cnm_221","cnm_h072","cnm_278"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 154","reason":"If the known angles are α and β, tan(α+β) = (3/4+5/12)/(1-(3/4)(5/12)) = 56/33. The third angle is complementary to their sum, so its tangent is 33/56. The primitive triple is (33,56,65), whose perimeter is 154.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-complement","label":"Tangent addition and complementary angle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-complement":["cnm_276","cnm_h072","cnm_116","cnm_174","cnm_p158"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_276","cnm_h072","cnm_116","cnm_174","cnm_p158"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c14d17c537a0827afe3c941c"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (7√3 - 12)","reason":"The minor arcs AB and AC are each 150°, and AD is 120°. Equality DG = AB, together with BG > GA, forces arc AG = 30°, making GC a diameter. Right-triangle angle relations give AF/AB = tan15° = 2 - √3 and AE/AC = √3tan15° = 2√3 - 3. Their product is 7√3 - 12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cyclic-angles-and-right-triangles","label":"Cyclic angles and right triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cyclic-angles-and-right-triangles":["cnm_254","cnm_278","cnm_h072","cnm_140"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_254","cnm_278","cnm_h072","cnm_140"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h072"],"label":"两角和与差的正切","layer":"CN","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"criteria":["能计算 tan(α−β)","知道分母为 0 时公式不能用"],"description":"会用正切的和差公式计算分母不为 0 的特殊值。","id":"cnm_h072","label":"两角和与差的正切","original":{"chapter":"高一 第五章 三角函数 5.5","description":"会用正切的和差公式计算分母不为 0 的特殊值。","domain":"数与代数","external":false,"grade":10,"id":"cnm_h072","mastery_criteria":["能计算 tan(α−β)","知道分母为 0 时公式不能用"],"name":"两角和与差的正切","semester":"上","standards":[{"confidence":"inferred","id":"cms-2017:H.FUN.16","note":"正切的和差公式属于三角恒等变换。待核。"}],"type":"procedural"},"placement":{"bookCode":"10","bookLabel":"必修一","chapter":"高一 第五章 三角函数 5.5","domain":"数与代数","semester":"上"},"reviewStatus":"source_only"},"sourceNodeId":"cnm_h072","status":"mapped","total":23}
